You can save a JavaScript object to `localStorage` by first serializing it into a JSON string using `JSON.stringify()`, and then using `localStorage.setItem()`. To read it back, use `localStorage.getItem()` and then parse the string back into an object with `JSON.parse()`. Here's an example:

```javascript
// Save object to localStorage
const myObject = { name: "John", age: 30 };
localStorage.setItem("myKey", JSON.stringify(myObject));

// Read object from localStorage
const retrievedObject = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("myKey"));
console.log(retrievedObject.name); // Outputs: John
```

This approach allows you to store and retrieve complex data structures in `localStorage`.
